UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 2: Making a timeline of our area', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A timeline puts events in order using dates on an evenly spaced scale. The distance between events shows how much time passed, so the spacing must match the dates.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nOn a line marked every 10 years from 1900 to 2020 place: school 1912, tram line 1930, park 1975, library 2005. How many years between the park and the library?\n\nFEEDBACK\nMark each date at its own position. The park and library are 30 years apart. The earliest gap is school to tram line, 18 years.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSpacing events evenly just to make them fit, regardless of their dates, gives a timeline that misleads the reader about time.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nBuild a timeline of six dated events from your area or family. Use one consistent scale, add a title and write one sentence about a pattern you can see.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"A timeline puts events in order using dates on an evenly spaced scale. The distance between events shows how much time passed, so the spacing must match the dates.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Build a timeline of six dated events from your area or family. Use one consistent scale, add a title and write one sentence about a pattern you can see. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example timeline for Riverside, one centimetre for every 20 years: school opens 1890, bridge built 1925, library opens 1968, skate park opens 2010. The gap between the school and the bridge is 35 years, so it is shorter than the gap between the bridge and the skate park.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Spacing events evenly just to make them fit, regardless of their dates, gives a timeline that misleads the reader about time. Correction: Mark each date at its own position. The park and library are 30 years apart. The earliest gap is school to tram line, 18 years.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Spacing events evenly just to make them fit, regardless of their dates, gives a timeline that misleads the reader about time. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A timeline puts events in order using dates on an evenly spaced scale. The distance between events shows how much time passed, so the spacing must match the dates. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["A timeline puts events in order using dates on an evenly spaced scale. The distance between events shows how much time passed, so the spacing must match the dates.","Spacing events evenly just to make them fit, regardless of their dates, gives a timeline that misleads the reader about time.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Spacing events evenly just to make them fit, regardless of their dates, gives a timeline that misleads the reader about time.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new inquiry\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Mark each date at its own position. The park and library are 30 years apart. The earliest gap is school to tram line, 18 years. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["Mark each date at its own position. The park and library are 30 years apart. The earliest gap is school to tram line, 18 years.","Spacing events evenly just to make them fit, regardless of their dates, gives a timeline that misleads the reader about time.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"On a line marked every 10 years from 1900 to 2020 place: school 1912, tram line \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Mark each date at its own position. The park and library are 30 years apart. The earliest gap is school to tram line, 18 years. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["Mark each date at its own position. The park and library are 30 years apart. The earliest gap is school to tram line, 18 years.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Spacing events evenly just to make them fit, regardless of their dates, gives a timeline that misleads the reader about time.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with e\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Inquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named sources or places.","question":"Inquire into or describe a fresh example that uses the central skill. Show evidence and name the sources or places that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Example timeline for Riverside, one centimetre for every 20 years: school opens 1890, bridge built 1925, library opens 1968, skate park opens 2010. The gap between the school and the bridge is 35 years, so it is shorter than the gap between the bridge and the skate park.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the claim or evidence, not just make the notes longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected claim and an evidence-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely misconception for this HSIE topic, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Spacing events evenly just to make them fit, regardless of their dates, gives a timeline that misleads the reader about time. Correction: Mark each date at its own position. The park and library are 30 years apart. The earliest gap is school to tram line, 18 years.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w03-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise timeline.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are 